      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"biological hierarchy\" side often argues that the cognitive and behavioral differences between racial groups are the result of adaptation to different environmental pressures throughout history. They claim that groups that had to adapt to more complex social environments, such as hunter-gatherer societies in Africa, developed more advanced cognitive abilities, such as language and cooperation. In contrast, groups that lived in more stable, agrarian environments, such as East Asia, developed more practical skills, such as agriculture and engineering.\n\nThey argue that the genetic differences between racial groups reflect these adaptations, with groups that had to adapt to more complex environments having more genetic variation in genes related to cognitive abilities, such as the BDNF gene, which is associated with intelligence. They also point to the fact that many of the genetic variants associated with intelligence are more common in populations that have a history of complex social organization, such as the Yoruba people of West Africa.\n\nFor example, they might argue that the high levels of cognitive ability in East Asians are due to their adaptation to the complex agricultural societies of ancient China, which required advanced mathematical and engineering skills to manage large populations and complex irrigation systems. Similarly, they might argue that the high levels of intelligence in Northern Europeans are due to their adaptation to",
